Elements of Information Security
CIA + AN
	-Confidentiality
		Who can access it?
    	Assurance that only authorized personnel have access
    	Ex: Only you can see your bank statement
	-Integrity
    	Was it changed?
    	The trustworthiness of data or resouces with unauthorized changes
    	Your bank balance isn't altered from ₹50,000 to ₹5,000
  	-Availability
		Can I access it when needed?
    	Systems/data should be accessible when needed
    	UPI/banking service works when you need to pay
	-Authenticity
    	Is it really who/what it claims to be?
    	Proves genuineness/identity of the sources
    	You verify that an email really came from your bank
  	-Non-repudiation
    	Can they deny doing it later?
    	Provides evidence that an action/message really occurred (Proof of action)
    	A digitally signed document proves who signed it

Information Security Attacks
	Attacks = Motive + Method + Vulnerability
		Motive - Goals 
		Method - TTPS 
			Tactics - Strategy followed
			Techniques - Technical method used
			Procedures - Systematic approach followed
		Vulnerability - Weakness/defect
			Common reasons:
				Hardware/Software misconfiguration
				Design flaw of network or application
				Inherent technology weakness
				End-user carelessness
				Intentional End-User act
			Examples:
				Technological:
					TCP/IP vulnerabilities - HTTP, FTP, ICMP, SNMP, SMTP, are inherently insecure
					OS vulnerabilities
					Network device vulnerabilities
				Configuration:
					User account vulnerabilities
					System account vulnerabilities
					Internet service misconfiguraton
					Default password settings
					Network device misconfiguration
	Classification of Attacks:
		Passive, Active, Close-in, Insider, Distribution
		Passive
			Footprinting
			Sniffing and eavesdropping
			Network traffic analysis
			Decryption of weak-encrypted traffic
		
			
